==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kelh₂-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|clergie}} (attested in the 13th century), from {{der|en|fro|clergié||learned men}}, from {{der|en|LL.|clēricātus}}, from {{der|en|la|clēricus||one ordained for religious services}}, from {{der|en|grc|κληρικός||of the clergy}}. Equivalent to {{suf|nocat=1|en|cleric|-ate|id2=rank or office}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklɜːdʒi/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈklɝd͡ʒi/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-clergy.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)dʒi|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Body of persons, such as [[priest]]s, who are trained and [[ordained]] for [[religious]] service.
#: {{ux|en|Today we brought together '''clergy''' from the Wiccan, Christian, New Age and Islamic traditions for an interfaith dialogue.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=5|passage=Then everybody once more knelt, and soon the blessing was pronounced. The choir and the '''clergy''' trooped out slowly, [&hellip;], down the nave to the western door. [&hellip;] At a seemingly immense distance the surpliced group stopped to say the last prayer.}}

